TEST 8770 -- IPv6 Neighbors - in silicon
===========================================================================
Run:    2026-09-30 17:39-18:05 NZDT (device log UTC 04:39-05:05), tb470, bench-runner
        (queue CAMPAIGN-QUEUE-2026-09-29.md row 11). Hand-driven: no framework script.
Build:  awplus_main-20260923-20
DUT:    IE520 3-member VCStack "IE520-stk" (members 1/3/4, master member 3), router
        (virtual) MAC 0000.cd37.0d6f
Case:   AWPTCM-T8770 (ck.db). 1) Use IxExplorer to emulate the MAXIMUM number of IPv6
        neighbours ("consult the limits database ... this is approximately 5000"), traffic
        to all of them sequentially at nearly 100 %: a) VLAN with an IPv6 address,
        b) connect the emulated hosts to it, c) verify the maximum number are learned
        (`show ipv6 neighbors`) and that traffic flows. 2) Check the learned neighbours
        are in hardware (`show platform` commands): "Able to learn maximum number of
        neighbors in hardware table".
        Replaces the 2026-09-22 UNMEASURED / 09-23 SKIPPED log
        (ipv6-2026-09-22/8770.log: "no ixia"). There is still no Ixia; the emulation is the
        T6057 responder method (switching-2026-09-22/6057.log), done for IPv6.

VERDICT: FAIL.
  The check in step 2, as written, fails: the DUT learns more IPv6 neighbours in software
  than its hardware table can hold, so learned neighbours are NOT in silicon. With traffic
  to 5000 neighbours:
    `show ipv6 neighbors`            3030 learned neighbours
    `show platform table ipv6`       1567 of them as /128 host entries (each stack member)
    `show log`                       "EXFX: Unable to add NH Route entry nh=<addr>/128 ...
                                     Table full", 3718 messages on all three members,
                                     1893 distinct neighbour addresses
    line-rate stream                 42-45 % of 970 Mbps delivered (40.2 % overall)
  The hardware limit measured here is ~1.9 K IPv6 neighbours (max 1904 /128 rows seen),
  against the case's "approximately 5000".
  PROVEN, and working: neighbours ARE learned by real ND and ARE programmed into silicon
  (identical on all three members) up to that limit, and traffic to them forwards at
  line rate (97-99 % of 984 Mbps at steady state for 2000 neighbours).
  FOR REVIEW:
  - The limits database was not available to this bench (no IE520 page in the AW+ wiki,
    nothing in ck.db). If the IE520's specified IPv6-neighbour limit is ~1.9-2 K,
    the capacity half of this FAIL goes. The mismatch half does not: neighbours beyond the
    hardware table are still admitted to software, and only a log warning shows it.
    Compare IPv4 (T6057): there the ARP table REFUSES new entries at 2045
    (ENOBUFS) instead of admitting entries it cannot program.

BENCH NOTES (how this was run)
  - Topology, all in the standing bench (no recable):
      source    tb470 eth2 -> IE520-sa port1.0.2 (vlan1) -> sa3 -> stack (vlan1, routed)
      neighbours stack port3.0.13 -> tb470 eth1, moved into scratch VLAN 3971
  - The emulated neighbours: nsresp.py on eth1 (evidence/tools; raw AF_PACKET socket,
    kernel BPF `icmp6 and ip6[40]==135`, promiscuous). It answers every NS whose
    target is in 2001:db8:8770:1::/64 with a solicited NA (S=1 O=1), from a unique MAC
    02:87:70:<low 24 bits of the target>, so the DUT also learns that MAC on port3.0.13.
  - Traffic: mkpcap.py pcaps (routed UDP to 2001:db8:8770:1::1:<n>, from
    2001:db8:8770:ff::2 to the stack MAC), replayed from eth2 with tcpreplay.
    Counted at eth1: tcpdump -w /dev/null on dst 02:87:70:* (exact, "0 packets dropped by
    kernel"), plus per-second sysfs counters (eth2 tx, eth1 rx).
  - tb470 has ip_forward=1 and IPv6 forwarding=1 (/proc/sys, read 17:34). So every receive-
    side host uses a FAKE locally-administered MAC: a frame to a real tb470 NIC MAC would
    be routed onward by the host.
  - Case-scope reading: step 1b's "Ixia ports" = one host NIC with thousands of emulated,
    RESPONDING neighbours. Needed because the DUT learns only from its own resolution
    (T6057: gratuitous ARP is ignored).

